    "done_when": "The review of the-clone-scan-reads-only-the-promoted-diff (92) found that reckon/clones.py never evicts a corpus cache entry: every content a file ever had keeps its entry, and _store_corpus_cache rewrites the whole corpus.json on each change. After one promotion on this host, ~/.cache/reckon/clones/corpus.json already held 867 entries in 14.4 MB. Several worktrees at different revisions share one cache root, so dropping every entry the current scan did not touch would evict a sibling revision's entries and make scans re-parse in turn. The cache records when each entry was last used and evicts the least recently used once it exceeds a stated cap. The cap is derived from the corpus file count, not a fixed literal, and is stated in the docstring with its reason. The second follow-on is folded in too: the window providers share one declared type (a Protocol or a common base) that _index and head_functions name, instead of list[Any]. tests/test_the_clone_cache_stays_bounded.py drives scans over more distinct revisions than the cap admits in a temporary cache root, and asserts the entry count stays at or below the cap. It also asserts that an entry used by the latest scan survives eviction and that the reported matches equal a cold full scan. The declared negative control is executed and logged. Base and head arms of tests/test_the_clone_scan_reads_only_the_promoted_diff.py, tests/test_promotion_reports_clones.py and tests/test_velocity_clone_matches.py show zero added failures.",
    "estimated_hours": 0.8,
    "goal": "The clone scan's on-disk corpus cache holds a bounded number of entries however many revisions share it",
